  data:  # b638
    kind: work_item  # b638
    title: "Reclassify ADR-0025 / ADR-0026 as audit-findings; add ADR-vs-audit-findings guidance to agentic infrastructure"  # b638
    status: needs_human_review  # b638
    priority: 3  # b638
    parent:  # b638
    tags:  # b638
    - concept_audit  # b638
    - agent_governance  # b638
    - developer_experience  # b638
    isbefore:  # b638
    - WI-dahim-vupif-lavin-kukor-gufih-gutad-kanuv-johor  # b638
    - WI-pilit-tokuh-nabij-fonin-sodis-salut-pibop-dirog  # b638
    duplicate_of: []  # b638
    not_duplicate_of: []  # b638
    pr_ref:  # b638
    description: "Two coupled deliverables: (A) physical reclassification of ADR-0025 + ADR-0026 out of the ADR namespace into an audit-findings series, and (B) prompting/guidance updates across the agentic infrastructure so future audit work files into the right series from the start (especially before ADR-0027 / ADR-0028 produce their own per-family follow-ons).\n\nCONTEXT — the categorical confusion:\n\nADR-0023 (Edge.edge_type axis principle) and ADR-0024 (axis-declaration meta-template) fit the canonical ADR mold: each is a single load-bearing decision with consequences. ADR-0025 and ADR-0026, by contrast, are largely TABLES OF PER-VALUE VERDICTS produced by applying the Fundamental Concept Audit playbook to specific families (dispatch+publish, IPC). Read literally, ADR-0025 is 13 verdicts arrived at by the four-leakage-test methodology; ADR-0026 is 7 verdicts in the same shape. The 'decision' in each isn't an architectural principle — it's a case ruling under existing law (ADR-0023 + ADR-0024). They're better classified as audit-findings documents.\n\nThis codebase has been comfortable treating 'any structurally-significant document with consequences for the code' as ADR-worthy. That's a permissive definition that produces three failure modes that have already shipped:\n  - Audit-finding documents with verdict tables (ADR-0025, ADR-0026).\n  - Analysis-snapshot documents with date-stamped titles (ADR-0003 original).\n  - Hybrid survey + design documents (ADR-0003-ext usage-context-patterns).\n\nThe trajectory matters: ADR-0027 (Symbol.kind axis) and ADR-0028 (evidence_type axis) are predicted to trigger 2-6 audit-findings follow-ons each (per the WI-dumiz-bikul / WI-turin-pajuk audit verdicts). If those ship under the ADR namespace, the ADR list will dilute further — discoverability suffers because a reader trying to understand 'what principles govern this codebase' gets a noisy mix of principles and rulings. Doing this cleanup AFTER 6+ more ADR-0025-shape entries land will be substantially more painful than doing it now.\n\nPART A — RECLASSIFY ADR-0025 + ADR-0026:\n\n1. Create new audit-findings series: docs/audits/<NN>-<topic>.md. Recommend numbering the new series independently from 0001. (Alternative: preserve the original numbers — docs/audits/0025-... — for inbound-reference stability. Choice deferred to human review.)\n\n2. Relocate file content. ADR-0025 → docs/audits/0001-dispatch-publish-family.md (or 0025-...). ADR-0026 → docs/audits/0002-ipc-family.md (or 0026-...). Add a top-level note in each new file: 'This document is an audit-findings record produced by applying the Fundamental Concept Audit playbook to a specific family scope. It records per-value verdicts; for the underlying axis principle see ADR-0023, and for the audit methodology see ADR-0024 § Enforcement and the audit playbook.'\n\n3. Leave a stub at the old ADR path: docs/adr/0025-... → 'RELOCATED — see docs/audits/<new-path>. ADR-0025 was reclassified per [WI-this-item] because it's an audit-findings document, not an architecture decision. Inbound references should now point at the new path.' Keep the stub forever; it's a discoverability redirect.\n\n4. Update inbound references. Search-and-replace pattern (verify before bulk-applying):\n   - ADR-0023 §6 cites both ADR-0025 and ADR-0026.\n   - hypergumbo_core.edge_types.EDGE_TYPES descriptions cite ADR-0025/0026 in many EdgeTypeSpec.description strings.\n   - CHANGELOG.md has multiple references.\n   - docs/migrating-edge-types.md cites both in headings and prose.\n   - docs/adr/README.md needs index update.\n\n5. Verify the registry's drift property test still passes (no expected impact; the relocation is pure documentation).\n\nPART B — GUIDANCE UPDATES TO PREVENT RECURRENCE:\n\nThe codebase needs explicit guidance about which document type to use for which deliverable shape. Three locations:\n\n1. docs/adr/README.md (ADR index): add a 'When to write an ADR vs an audit-findings document' section with concrete heuristics. Suggested rubric:\n   - Write an ADR when the deliverable is a load-bearing principle, a meta-template, or a single architectural decision with code-level consequences. Examples: ADR-0023 (principle), ADR-0024 (template).\n   - Write an audit-findings document (docs/audits/<NN>.md) when the deliverable is a TABLE of per-value verdicts produced by applying an existing methodology (especially the Fundamental Concept Audit playbook) to a specific scope. Examples: ADR-0025/0026 reclassified per [this item].\n   - Write a survey or analysis-snapshot document (docs/surveys/<topic>.md or docs/architecture-snapshots/<date>.md) when the deliverable is primarily a catalog or point-in-time inventory, even if it informs decisions. ADR-0003 original (Architectural Analysis — January 7, 2026) and ADR-0003-ext (usage-context-patterns) have hybrid shapes that would benefit from this split, but reclassifying them is OUT OF SCOPE for this item — file as separate follow-up if desired.\n\n2. .agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/what-if-we-dont-know-what-the-fuck-we-are-talking-about-audit-aka-fundamental-concept-audit.md: Step 6 ('Document outcome') currently doesn't specify WHERE the verdict artifact lives. Add:\n   'If the outcome is a single principle or meta-template, file as docs/adr/<NN>-<topic>.md. If the outcome is per-value verdicts for a family or scope (the typical Step 5 Adjacent Concept Sweep follow-on shape), file as docs/audits/<NN>-<topic>.md, citing the parent ADR that establishes the principle. ADR-0025 and ADR-0026 were originally filed as ADRs in error and have been reclassified.'\n\n3. AGENTS.md (governance — requires human approval, hence this item's needs_human_review status): the Architecture & Context section or the Required Checks section should gain a brief 'ADR vs audit-findings filing convention' note, with a pointer at docs/adr/README.md's expanded rubric. One-paragraph essentialization is enough; the rubric lives in the README.\n\n4. ADR-0024 § Enforcement: clarifies that family-audit outputs (the per-pair verdict tables an axis declaration tends to produce as it migrates through Phase 3) file as audit-findings documents, not as new ADRs. Cite ADR-0025/0026 (post-relocation) as worked examples.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n- ADR-0025 and ADR-0026 relocated to docs/audits/<NN>.md per the human-approved choice in PART A item 1.\n- Old ADR paths leave a stub redirect.\n- Inbound references in ADR-0023, EDGE_TYPES descriptions, CHANGELOG, docs/migrating-edge-types.md, docs/adr/README.md updated.\n- docs/adr/README.md gains a 'When to write an ADR vs audit-findings vs survey/snapshot' rubric.\n- The audit playbook's Step 6 specifies the audit-findings filing path.\n- AGENTS.md gains a one-paragraph filing-convention note pointing at the README rubric.\n- ADR-0024 § Enforcement names the audit-findings filing path as the correct landing for family-audit outputs.\n- ADR-0027 / ADR-0028 (the next ADRs in flight, per WI-dahim and WI-pilit) get filed under the new convention from the start: per-family verdicts go to docs/audits/, the axis-declaration ADRs themselves stay under docs/adr/.\n\nDEPENDENCIES:\n- HARD: this item is isbefore WI-dahim (ADR-0027 draft) and WI-pilit (ADR-0028 draft). If those ship first, their follow-on per-family verdicts will likely cement the old ADR-as-audit-findings pattern further, making the cleanup costlier.\n- governance: AGENTS.md edit triggers the AGENTS.md governance approval workflow per the project rules — hence this item's needs_human_review status.\n\nOUT OF SCOPE (file separately if desired):\n- Reclassifying ADR-0003 (original) and/or ADR-0003-ext (usage-context-patterns) — both have hybrid analysis-snapshot/decision shapes that would benefit from a split, but the cost/benefit is different than ADR-0025/0026 (older, more inbound references, and the catalog content is less cleanly separable from the decision content).\n- Renumbering scheme migration for the existing ADR series (e.g., shifting all post-0024 numbers down to absorb the freed 0025/0026 slots). Stubs at the old paths preserve discoverability without renumbering.\n\nBACKGROUND: surfaced when the human asked whether ADR-0025/0026 are 'playbooks masquerading as ADRs' (closer to audit-findings than playbooks, but yes — categorically distinct from ADR-0023/0024). The audit was extended to find older ADRs in the same shape; ADR-0003 lineage surfaced as a partial hybrid case but is out of scope here."  # b638
    fields: {}  # b638
- op: discuss  # 3471
  at: '2026-05-01T21:23:22Z'  # 3471
  by: agent  # 3471
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3471
  clock: 2  # 3471
  nonce: '3471'  # 3471
  message: "SCOPE EXPANSION (per same-turn user clarification): also revise ADR-0024 itself as part of this item, not just add a one-paragraph enforcement note.\n\nWhy ADR-0024 needs a more substantial revision (not just an enforcement-note addition):\n\nADR-0024's current shape implicitly treats the seven-step axis-declaration workflow as 'one ADR per axis'. The per-family audit verdicts that come out of working a Phase 3 migration through (which is what ADR-0025 and ADR-0026 are) are framed in ADR-0024 as if they're additional ADRs. Recasting them as audit-findings documents requires changing how ADR-0024 talks about the workflow's outputs:\n\nCONCRETE ADR-0024 EDITS in scope of this item:\n\n1. The seven-step workflow's per-step language. Wherever a step's output is described as 'a follow-on ADR' or similar (especially around per-family audits during Phase 3), change to 'an audit-findings document at docs/audits/<NN>.md'. The axis-declaration ADR itself is one document; per-family verdicts that fall out during migration are separate documents in a different series.\n\n2. § Enforcement: explicit statement that family-audit outputs file as audit-findings, not as new ADRs. (This is the same thing I had in PART B item 4 of the original description, but with ADR-0024 being substantively revised, the language needs to fit the new shape rather than be tacked on.)\n\n3. Worked-example pointer: where ADR-0024 currently cites ADR-0023 as the worked example, also cite the relocated docs/audits/<NN>-dispatch-publish-family.md and docs/audits/<NN>-ipc-family.md as worked examples of the audit-findings outputs that the seven-step workflow tends to produce. Distinguish: ADR-0023 = the axis-declaration ADR (Step 1-2 output); the audits = the family-verdict documents (Step 5-6 output during Phase 3 migration).\n\n4. Status section: confirm Accepted, possibly add an 'updated YYYY-MM-DD' footnote noting the audit-findings filing-path clarification. Don't supersede the ADR; just refine.\n\nWhy same-item rather than separate item: ADR-0024's revision is structurally inseparable from the reclassification work — the relocation of ADR-0025/0026 makes the current ADR-0024 text wrong, so they must land together. Splitting would create a temporary inconsistency window where ADR-0024's shipped text contradicts the actual filing convention.\n\nACCEPTANCE (extends Part B):\n- ADR-0024 substantively revised (not just one paragraph appended) to reflect the ADR-vs-audit-findings distinction throughout the seven-step workflow and § Enforcement.\n- ADR-0024 cites the relocated audit-findings docs as worked examples alongside ADR-0023's axis-declaration ADR worked example.\n\nThe item's HARD isbefore on WI-dahim and WI-pilit applies to this expanded scope too — those will be the second and third axis-declaration ADRs to instantiate ADR-0024's revised template, so the template needs to be coherent before they ship."  # 3471
- op: discuss  # 90aa
  at: '2026-05-01T21:28:25Z'  # 90aa
  by: agent  # 90aa
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 90aa
  clock: 3  # 90aa
  nonce: 90aa  # 90aa
  message: "SCOPE EXPANSION (per same-turn user clarification): the audit-findings document format MUST include per-verdict compliance state.\n\nWhy: an audit-findings document is a LIVING artifact in a way typical ADRs aren't. Each verdict in the document (FOLD, DEPRECATE-NO-FOLD, CANONICAL) is a remediation directive whose compliance changes over time as migration phases ship. ADR-0023 captured a principle that's load-bearing the moment it ships; ADR-0025-shape verdicts are promises that may or may not have been kept yet. The audit-findings doc is the right home for the compliance status because the verdicts and the status are the same artifact viewed at different times.\n\nPER-VERDICT COMPLIANCE STATE — proposed lifecycle:\n\n- DECIDED: verdict recorded in the audit; no migration work shipped yet.\n- MIGRATED (awaits validation): Phase 3 producer migration shipped; awaits_bakeoff_validation tag still active on the corresponding tracker item.\n- VALIDATED: bakeoff cleared the tag; value still in the registry pending its Phase 4b removal.\n- PRUNED: Phase 4b shipped; value removed from registry; consumer-side enumerations cleaned up.\n\n(For DEPRECATE-NO-FOLD verdicts the lifecycle compresses: the producer drop is itself the migration; PRUNED applies once the registry value is gone.)\n\nFORMAT — per-verdict status column in the verdicts table. Each row of the audit's central rename / verdict table gets a Status column. As of the relocation moment (this item's PR), all rows of ADR-0025 and ADR-0026 will read PRUNED (Phase 4b shipped at SCHEMA_VERSION 0.4.0 cleared every value in both audits' scope). The format starts to earn its keep on the NEXT round of audit-findings docs — e.g., the protocol-call audit (WI-vumum's downstream output) will cycle through DECIDED → MIGRATED → VALIDATED → PRUNED across multiple PRs, and a reader looking at the doc gets the current state directly instead of cross-referencing tracker items + CHANGELOG + registry.\n\nMAINTENANCE: prefer manual-with-validation over auto-generation:\n- The status field is hand-edited as each phase ships.\n- A pre-commit / property test verifies the status matches the actual codebase state. Concretely:\n  - PRUNED ↔ value is absent from EDGE_TYPES.\n  - MIGRATED ↔ value is in the registry as endpoint_shape AND no production emit site exists (drift linter coverage).\n  - VALIDATED ↔ MIGRATED + the corresponding tracker item lacks awaits_bakeoff_validation tag.\n  - DECIDED ↔ value is in registry as endpoint_shape AND production emit sites still exist.\n- If hand-edit drifts from reality, the test fails. Same shape as the schema-freshness and concept-axes-freshness checks already in place.\n\nGUIDANCE-DOC IMPLICATIONS (extends Part B):\n- docs/adr/README.md rubric: when the deliverable is per-value verdicts, file as audit-findings AND include the compliance-state column / format described here.\n- Fundamental Concept Audit playbook Step 6: when documenting outcome as audit-findings, include the per-verdict state column from the start; note the four-state lifecycle.\n- ADR-0024 § Enforcement: name compliance-state-tracked audit-findings docs as the canonical output of Phase 3 / Phase 4b migration work. Tracking is structural, not optional.\n\nACCEPTANCE (extends Part B):\n- Relocated docs/audits/<NN>-dispatch-publish-family.md and docs/audits/<NN>-ipc-family.md include a Status column on every verdict row.\n- Status values for ADR-0025/0026's verdicts at relocation moment: all PRUNED (Phase 4b complete for both audits' entire scope).\n- Property test (or pre-commit check) verifies the Status column is consistent with codebase state — i.e., PRUNED rows have their value absent from the registry, MIGRATED rows have their value in registry-as-endpoint_shape with zero producer emits, etc.\n- The audit-findings format documented in docs/adr/README.md or a sibling docs/audits/README.md establishing the format for future audits."  # 90aa
- op: discuss  # de50
  at: '2026-05-01T21:36:02Z'  # de50
  by: agent  # de50
  actor: jgstern_agent  # de50
  clock: 4  # de50
  nonce: de50  # de50
  message: "SCOPE CORRECTION (per same-turn user clarification): three-state lifecycle, not four. The previous four-state model (DECIDED / MIGRATED / VALIDATED / PRUNED) conflated remediation progress with bakeoff validation. Bakeoff validation is already tracked orthogonally via the awaits_bakeoff_validation tag on the related tracker item — duplicating it in the audit-findings doc adds no information.\n\nREVISED PER-VERDICT COMPLIANCE STATE — three-state lifecycle:\n\n- UNRESOLVED: verdict recorded; producers still emit the value.\n- PRELIM_RESOLVED: producer migration shipped; value remains in registry through the deprecation window.\n- RESOLVED: registry pruned; consumer-side enumerations cleaned up.\n\nThe PRELIM_RESOLVED state spans the full dual-validity window. Whether the migration has been bakeoff-validated within that window is tracked separately by the awaits_bakeoff_validation tag on the migration's tracker item, not by an additional audit-findings state.\n\nREVISED MECHANICAL CHECKS (replaces the four-state version):\n\n- UNRESOLVED ↔ value is in registry as endpoint_shape AND production emit sites exist (drift linter coverage; emit-site grep in non-test producer code).\n- PRELIM_RESOLVED ↔ value is in registry as endpoint_shape AND zero production emit sites.\n- RESOLVED ↔ value is absent from registry.\n\nFor DEPRECATE-NO-FOLD verdicts (e.g., event_subscribes, message_receive) the lifecycle compresses naturally: the producer drop is the migration (UNRESOLVED → PRELIM_RESOLVED), then registry removal is RESOLVED. Same three states apply.\n\nPROPERTY TEST: same shape as the schema-freshness and concept-axes-freshness pre-commit checks. For each verdict row in each audit-findings doc, the test reads the Status field and asserts the corresponding mechanical-check predicate holds. Hand-edits that drift from reality fail loudly.\n\nACCEPTANCE (replaces the prior four-state acceptance):\n- Audit-findings docs include a Status column with one of {UNRESOLVED, PRELIM_RESOLVED, RESOLVED} per verdict row.\n- Status values for the relocated ADR-0025/0026 docs at the moment of relocation: all RESOLVED (Phase 4b at SCHEMA_VERSION 0.4.0 cleared every value in both audits' scope).\n- Property test verifies each Status is consistent with the codebase state per the mechanical-check predicates.\n- Bakeoff validation status is NOT duplicated in the audit-findings doc; the awaits_bakeoff_validation tag on the migration's tracker item is the single source of truth for that orthogonal concern.\n- docs/adr/README.md rubric and the audit-findings format guidance specify the three-state lifecycle (not four) and the orthogonal-tag convention for bakeoff validation."  # de50
